  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nNear Term Through Friday Morning...\nSurface low pressure is currently centered along the Mid- \nAtlantic coast, with an inverted surface trough extending \nwestward across southern Illinois. As the primary low lifts \nnorth toward New England on Friday, this trough will pivot \nnorthward across central Illinois, maintaining periods of rain \nthrough tonight. Rain will taper off from west to east Friday \nmorning as the low pulls away and weak surface ridging builds in\nbehind it.\n\nThermal profiles support rain as the dominant precipitation \ntype, with 900-800mb layer maximum wet-bulb temperatures \nremaining between 0 and +3C and surface temperatures staying \nabove freezing overnight. Consequently, frozen precipitation is \nnot expected. The NBM 24-hour mean QPF through noon Friday \nindicates around 1 to 1.5 inches just east of the I-55 corridor,\nwith amounts rapidly diminishing to 0.10 inches or less along \nthe Illinois River Valley.\n\nWeekend System & Temperature Trend...\nA brief break from the rain is expected late Friday through \nSaturday as surface ridging crosses the region. Backing \nsouthwesterly winds will initiate a gradual warming trend, \nboosting high temperatures from the mid 40s on Friday to near 50\ndegrees on Saturday.\n\nMeanwhile, a fast-moving shortwave digging from the Pacific \nNorthwest on Saturday morning will reach the Southern Plains by \nSaturday night, deepening as it rounds the base of the longwave \ntrough. In response, a surface low will develop and track from \neastern Oklahoma Saturday night across southern Illinois by \nSunday morning. The GFS and ECMWF are in good agreement \nregarding the timing and evolution of this wave, bringing \nwidespread rain back to central Illinois. With surface \ntemperatures in the upper 30s to lower 40s Saturday night and \npersistent warm air aloft, rain will again be the primary \nprecipitation type. NBM 24-hour mean QPF ranges from around 0.50\ninches near Galesburg in the northwest to just under 1.00 inch \nsouth of I-70.\n\nNext Week Outlook...\nAs the system departs to the east late Sunday, upper-level flow\nwill transition back to zonal while an expansive surface high \npressure ridge builds over the Great Plains and moves into the \nMississippi Valley by Monday. Returning southwesterly flow\nbehind the ridge will support a warming trend through the first\nhalf of next week, with highs in the lower to mid 40s on Monday\nclimbing into the mid 50s by Wednesday.",
